      "math_academy_id": "q-128372",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(7,4,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨7,4,-3⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,4,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨5,4,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =⟨7,4,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =57 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z & =57 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z-57 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
